Current original Zero workflow (0.12)

Use the native tools on Linux or their .exe versions on Windows:
  zero-integrate PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-build PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-check PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_TEST_DIRECTORY
All destination paths must be new. The integrator preserves authored sources;
the builder checks its compiler fixed point and builds the original targets;
the verifier checks language behavior and host file operations in process.
See tools/integrate.ztext, tools/build.ztext and checks/README.ztext.
Read these documents with zero-text read FILE.

The notes below preserve earlier implementation and test history. Any old
shell/Python runner names, generated artifacts, JSON reports or commands are
historical references: they were removed from the active project and are not
current runnable instructions or current-release validation. Native host
checks do not replace BIOS/UEFI, persistence or physical-hardware testing.

Historical implementation notes

Zero volume discovery (original Zero implementation)

Entry150 examines the four IDENTIFY-populated legacy ATA positions, builds
A:| through Z:| in physical-device order and then partition-table order, and
returns199. A missing/ATAPI/incompatible physical device is skipped. No write
operation is issued; discovery neither formats nor repairs a disk.

Physical descriptors at H+896+i*32 contain capacity, validATA, discovery
status, partition kind, all u64. Status0 success,1 unsupported,2 corrupt or
ambiguous,3 I/O failure,6 inventory capacity exceeded. Kind0 raw,1 MBR,2 GPT.
The 26 volume descriptors at H+256+i*24 contain startLBA, sectorCount and
packed bytes: physical device at16, format17, later probe status18, write
blocked19. Filesystem probing is separate. GPT read-only attribute bit60
sets byte19 and must never be cleared by a filesystem probe.
H+128 holds volumeCount and H+144 the scan index. Other common request fields
are preserved. Private continuation state occupies25600..26623; buffers are
32768 and49152. Sector size512 and LBA28 capacity bounds are required.

Supported discovery profiles
- Raw whole-device volumes. Recognized ZeroFS and NTFS boot sectors bypass
  MBR interpretation; their bytes446..509 may contain ordinary filesystem
  boot code. Other raw volumes require no signed partition table and no
  partition-like records in that region. An EFI PART header without a valid
  protective MBR is rejected. A signed empty MBR has no exposed volumes.
- MBR: up to four primary partitions. Active flag0/0x80, nonzero start/size,
  physical bounds and pairwise disjoint intervals are required. CHS values
  are not used. Extended types05/0F/85 and hybrid protective tables are
  explicitly unsupported. Empty entries must have zero start and length.
- GPT revision1.0:128-byte entries,1..128 entries,512-byte header sector;
  headerSize92..512, valid primary and backup header CRC32, same disk GUID,
  usable bounds, entry count/size/CRC, opposite current/backup LBAs, and
  byte-identical primary/backup entry arrays. Table CRC covers precisely the
  declared entry bytes, not sector padding. Primary header is LBA1; backup
  header is the physical last sector. Both arrays must be outside usable
  data and inside disk bounds. Used entries require a nonzero unique GUID,
  ordered bounded first/last LBAs and no partition overlap. Empty type GUIDs
  denote unused entries. Partition names and type-specific attributes other
  than read-only bit60 do not alter discovery. Filesystem drivers validate
  the actual contained format independently.

A failed disk scan rolls back every provisional volume from that physical
disk while retaining valid volumes from other disks. More than26 volumes
never causes a truncated partial table to become writable: the overflowing
disk is rejected with status6. Letters are deterministic for the current
connected-device/table order; persistent letter assignment is not implemented.

Validation
review-tests/discovery/run.sh compiles the exact scanner into a hosted Zero
harness. The I/O adapter rejects writes and out-of-range accesses, deliberately
clobbers all volatile scalars, and verifies inventory memory canaries. Every
scratch-image checksum remains unchanged. The CRC helper independently checks
the standard123456789 -> CBF43926 vector. Original Zero fixture generation
covers raw/NTFS/ZeroFS, validMBR/GPT, malformed bounds and headers, primary and
backup CRC errors, mismatched arrays, overlapping partitions, duplicate GUIDs,
read-only attribute, unsupported tables and capacity rollback. Host partx
independently verifies the valid MBR/GPT fixture start/length pairs. These
are hosted parser tests; integrated guest boot tests are reported separately.
